        "@value": "src/resources/prompts/ontology.md"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Source prompt content at src/resources/prompts/ontology.md, linked to its consuming step and protected by a source fingerprint."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#definitionStatus": [
      {
        "@value": "source-derived description"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resourceKind": [
      {
        "@value": "prompt"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resourceText": [
      {
        "@value": "You place {{domain}} concepts in a BFO 2020 / Common Core Ontologies (CCO) class hierarchy.\n\nFor each input concept choose exactly one parent:\n- \"U:<label>\" = one of the concept's \"candidates\" (CCO and BFO classes retrieved for that concept from the ontology store, most specific first), or a category from BFO CATEGORIES, or\n- a concept id (k<n>) from CORPUS CONCEPTS, when the concept is truly a kind of that concept (every instance of the child is an instance of the parent).\nPick the most specific correct parent. Choose a corpus concept over an external class whenever a true is-a holds. Never invent a class that is not listed.\n\nBFO guidance:\n- material entity: things with mass ({{ex:material_entities}}); an amount of a substance or an engineered object goes under the candidate that names that kind of material entity.\n- quality: a measurable property that inheres in a bearer ({{ex:onto_quality}}). This is the property itself, not a measured value.\n- disposition / function: a capacity realized in processes ({{ex:onto_function}}).\n- process: something that unfolds in time ({{ex:onto_process}}).\n- information (generically dependent continuant): data, models, metrics as information, measurement values, standards, test protocols and plans.\n- A quality never goes under a material entity. A process never goes under a continuant. A defect that is a physical feature (crack, void) is a material entity or site; a defect that is a tendency is a disposition.\n\nSet \"exclude\": true only for items that should not be classes: a specific sample or dataset name, a single numeric value, a person or organization, a paper-specific label.\n\nReturn ONLY JSON matching the schema.\n\nSCHEMA\n\nUse source_definitions and relationship evidence as context, not universal definitions. Distinguish a general concept from an experimental configuration or particular specimen. Prefer a corpus parent supported by verified is_a evidence when categories agree; part_of and made_of are not is_a. Flagged or conditional source claims require caution; quote verification alone does not establish universal truth.\n"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#sourceDigest": [
      {
        "@value": "07a1c01ddef1459a660d8f35a44be9410f1cbea8a279028bcec3f3e070d08dd8"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#sourcePath": [
      {
        "@value": "src/resources/prompts/ontology.md"
      }
    ]
  },
