      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Source prompt content at src/resources/prompts/extraction_concepts.md, linked to its consuming step and protected by a source fingerprint."
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#definitionStatus": [
      {
        "@value": "source-derived description"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resourceKind": [
      {
        "@value": "prompt"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resourceText": [
      {
        "@value": "You identify EVERY domain concept in ONE SECTION of a {{domain}} research paper, and which listed figures show them. Be thorough. Be faithful: use only what this text says, in the paper's own words; never add background knowledge. Return ONLY one JSON object that matches the schema at the end. No prose.\n\nCONCEPTS - extract ALL of these that the section mentions:\n- materials and material forms ({{ex:materials}});\n- devices, device parts and structures ({{ex:devices}});\n- equipment and instruments ({{ex:equipment}});\n- process steps ({{ex:process_steps}});\n- process parameters ({{ex:parameters}});\n- measured properties and quantities ({{ex:properties}});\n- phenomena, mechanisms, defects and failure modes ({{ex:phenomena}});\n- characterization and analysis methods ({{ex:methods}});\n- conditions and stressors ({{ex:stressors}});\n- information artifacts (standards, datasets, models, metrics).\nSkip only generic words (study, result, approach, figure), people, institutions and citations.\n- label: a singular noun phrase as written in the paper. Lowercase, except acronyms, chemical formulas and proper names. Keep specific qualifiers ({{ex:label_example}}).\n- If CONCEPTS ALREADY FOUND is given, reuse those exact labels for the same things.\n- type: from the enum. process step -> process; process parameter -> parameter; measured quantity -> property or quantity.\n- synonyms: acronyms and alternate names used in the text.\n- definition: one sentence only if the text defines or clearly characterizes it; otherwise \"\".\n- figures: ids (F<n>) of the listed figures that show this concept.\n- ids: c1, c2, ... unique within your answer.\n\nRELATIONS (non-causal) - every stated or directly implied link: composition (made_of, has_part, part_of), is_a, has_property, measured_by, process order (precedes), inputs/outputs (input_of, output_of), used_for, located_in, participates_in.\n- s and o MUST be concept ids (c1, c2, ...) from CONCEPTS, never phrases; p from the enum; evidence = verbatim quote of at most 20 words.\n\nFIGURES - for each F<n> listed: the concept ids it depicts and what it shows in at most 12 words.\n\nSCHEMA\n"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#sourceDigest": [
      {
        "@value": "9f18e905c334f9485e591747a8169af4c2af033229ea42f8e33534a821a148e5"
      }
    ],
    "https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#sourcePath": [
      {
        "@value": "src/resources/prompts/extraction_concepts.md"
      }
    ]
  },
